    shortDescription: "Common / high-frequency Python interview question. Difficulty: Intermediate / Advanced Interview Focus: Problem Solving / Design / Scenario Best Answer Strategy: Clarify the requirement, state the simplest correct approach, show representative code, analyze complexity, then discuss edge cases and production considerations. ——————————————————————————–. Focus: .",
    explanation: "This is a common / high-frequency Python interview question. The supplied answer emphasizes accurate Python behavior, practical reasoning, relevant trade-offs, and interview-ready examples. The source does not claim that the question is guaranteed for every interview.",
    sampleAnswer: "An object is hashable when it has a hash value that remains stable during its lifetime and supports equality consistently. Hashable objects can be dictionary keys or set members. If __eq__ is customized, Python may make the class unhashable unless a suitable __hash__ is provided. Do not make mutable equality keys hashable if their hash can change while stored in a set or dictionary.",
    detailedAnswer: "Source classification: COMMON / HIGH-FREQUENCY\n\nAnswer:\nAn object is hashable when it has a hash value that remains stable during its lifetime and supports equality consistently. Hashable objects can be dictionary keys or set members. If __eq__ is customized, Python may make the class unhashable unless a suitable __hash__ is provided. Do not make mutable equality keys hashable if their hash can change while stored in a set or dictionary.\n\nExample:\nclass User: def init(self, id): self.id = id def eq(self, other): return isinstance(other, User) and self.id == other.id def hash(self): return hash(self.id)\n\nInterview Tip:\nDo not jump directly to code. Explain assumptions, the approach, complexity, edge cases, failure modes, and production trade-offs.\n\nWhat Interviewer Is Testing:\nWhether you can reason about correctness, scalability, concurrency, reliability, and maintainability.\n\nKey Points to Remember:\nAnswer the exact question first. - Explain behavior and reasoning, not just syntax. - Mention the most relevant edge case or trade-off.\n\nCommon Follow-up Questions:\nWhat is the time/space complexity? What happens at production scale? What are the failure or concurrency edge cases?\n\nCommon Mistakes:\nIgnoring edge cases, complexity, failure handling, or production constraints.\n\nDifficulty: Intermediate / Advanced Interview Focus: Problem Solving / Design / Scenario Best Answer Strategy: Clarify the requirement, state the simplest correct approach, show representative code, analyze complexity, then discuss edge cases and production considerations. ——————————————————————————–\nInterview Focus: \nBest Answer Strategy:\n\n\nSource basis note: The supplied source states that its source basis and classification are retained and that the questions are not guaranteed to appear in every interview.",
